# Engineering Rules for ToDone

## Language & Monorepo Environment
- **Platform**: TypeScript (strict mode), Node.js (>= 22), pnpm workspaces (`packages/core`, `packages/adapters`, `packages/acceptance`, `apps/todone`).
- **Dependency Rule**: Source code dependencies must point only inward toward pure business logic.
- `packages/core` must have ZERO external dependencies on frameworks (React, Expo), databases (SQLite, IndexedDB), or network libraries.
- All external dependencies and storage implementations live in `packages/adapters` or `apps/todone`.
- Verify workspace integrity with `pnpm -r typecheck` and `pnpm test`.

## Acceptance Pipeline Specification (APS)
- Acceptance criteria must be authored in declarative Gherkin `.feature` files located in `packages/acceptance/features/`.
- Conforms to Uncle Bob's `parser-spec.md` in `unclebob/Acceptance-Pipeline-Specification`.
- Acceptance tests must execute against **Use Case Interactor boundaries** via interface adapter fixtures (`TaskFixture`), completely decoupled from the UI and live database.
- Acceptance tests must run in sub-second time with zero flakiness.
- Scenarios must use parameter placeholders (`<param>`) and `Examples:` tables for data variations.

## Test-Driven Development (TDD)
- Coder must work test-first: write a failing unit or acceptance test expressing the desired behavior before writing production code.
- Write only enough production code to pass the tests.
- Refactor continuously while maintaining all tests passing.
- Do not bypass type checks (`@ts-ignore` is prohibited).

## Design & Testability
- Work in small, reviewable increments.
- Prefer deep modules with simple, expressive interfaces.
- Separate testable core domain logic from environmentally unsuitable modules (native mobile APIs, file system, UI animations).
- IO-near modules must not reimplement domain rules. When UI or adapters need an answer, call the core use case interactor.
